@*
    One civic or origin, said in full.

    A card rather than a tile, because this is a page to read rather than a picker to choose from:
    the name and the icon say which one it is, and everything under them is the answer to a question
    somebody came here with. What it does, who may have it, and what it costs in packs.

    Not a button. Nothing on this page is selectable - pressing a civic in a wiki should do nothing,
    and a tile that looks pressable and is not is worse than one that does not.
*@

<article class="sem-wiki-card @(Row.Reach.EverOffered ? null : "unreachable")">
    <header>
        <GameIcon Path="@Row.Icon" Alt="@Row.Name" />

        <div class="title">
            <h2>@Row.Name</h2>

            <div class="sem-wiki-badges">
                @*
                    Two different kinds of "you cannot have this", and they are worth telling apart.
                    A pack is something you could buy; a fallen empire's civic is not for sale.
                *@
                @if (Row.ClosedShort is { } closed)
                {
                    <span class="sem-badge closed" title="@Row.ClosedWhy">@closed</span>
                }

                @foreach (var pack in Row.Packs)
                {
                    <span class="sem-badge @(Row.Owned ? "pack" : "pack missing")"
                          title="@(Row.Owned ? $"Needs {pack.Name}, which you have" : $"Needs {pack.Name}, which you do not have")">
                        @pack.Name
                    </span>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </header>

    @if (Row.Description is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <p class="sem-wiki-prose">@((MarkupString)Session.Localizer.Html($"{Row.Key}_desc", string.Empty))</p>
    }

    <EffectsList Effects="@Row.Effects" />

    @*
        All five conditions, each under its own heading, because the game means something different
        by each and keeps them apart: what you must own, what your empire must already be for this to
        appear, what makes it legal once it does, and the two halves of whether a government reform
        can change its mind later. A reader mid-game asking "can I still get this" is asking about
        the fourth, and running them together would lose them the answer.
    *@
    <dl class="sem-wiki-conditions">
        @foreach (var condition in Row.Conditions)
        {
            <div class="@(condition.Stated ? "stated" : null)">
                <dt>@condition.Heading</dt>
                <dd>@condition.Sentence</dd>
            </div>
        }
    </dl>
</article>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The civic or origin to draw.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required CivicRow Row { get; set; }
}
